// Statement: static SQL (OK for no user input)
Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM config");

// PreparedStatement: parameterized (ALWAYS for user input)
PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(
    "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?");
ps.setInt(1, userId);

// CallableStatement: stored procedure
CallableStatement cs = conn.prepareCall("{call get_user(?)}");
cs.setInt(1, userId);

// VULNERABLE: attacker enters: ' OR '1'='1
String username = request.getParam("user"); // ' OR '1'='1
String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username='" + username + "'";
// Becomes: WHERE username='' OR '1'='1' -- matches all rows!
Statement stmt = conn.createStatement();
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(sql); // DANGER!

// SAFE: PreparedStatement
PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(
    "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username=?");
ps.setString(1, username); // treated as data, not SQL

// Call stored procedure with IN and OUT params
CallableStatement cs = conn.prepareCall("{call get_employee_salary(?, ?)}");

// Set IN parameter
cs.setInt(1, employeeId);

// Register OUT parameter
cs.registerOutParameter(2, Types.DECIMAL);

cs.execute();

// Get OUT parameter value
double salary = cs.getDouble(2);

// SELECT
ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();

// INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE
int rowsAffected = ps.executeUpdate();

// Dynamic SQL (unknown type)
boolean hasResultSet = stmt.execute(sql);
if (hasResultSet) {
    ResultSet rs = stmt.getResultSet();
}

**Use Statement for:**
```java
// DDL — no user input possible
statement.execute("CREATE TABLE temp_report AS SELECT * FROM orders WHERE 1=0");
statement.execute("CREATE INDEX idx_email ON users(email)");

// Fixed admin queries — no parameters
statement.executeQuery("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM information_schema.tables");

// One-off scripts — not application code
statement.executeUpdate("DELETE FROM session_tokens WHERE expires_at < NOW()");
```

**Always use PreparedStatement for:**
```java
// Any query with values from variables
preparedStatement.setString(1, userInput); // could be anything

// Even constants from config — use PreparedStatement for consistency
// It's easier to review code that always uses PS
```

**Why PreparedStatement even when injection isn't a risk:**
- Performance: query plan cached and reused
- Code clarity: ? clearly marks where data enters the query
- Consistency: team always uses PS, reviews for Statement catch anomalies

**Procedure with IN and OUT parameters:**
```sql
-- PostgreSQL example
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE transfer_funds(
    IN from_account INT, IN to_account INT, IN amount DECIMAL,
    OUT success BOOLEAN
)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - amount WHERE id = from_account;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + amount WHERE id = to_account;
    success := TRUE;
END;
$$;
```

```java
try (CallableStatement cs = conn.prepareCall(
        "{call transfer_funds(?, ?, ?, ?)}")) {

    cs.setInt(1, fromAccountId);        // IN
    cs.setInt(2, toAccountId);          // IN
    cs.setBigDecimal(3, amount);        // IN
    cs.registerOutParameter(4, Types.BOOLEAN); // OUT

    cs.execute();

    boolean ok = cs.getBoolean(4); // read OUT parameter
    System.out.println("Transfer successful: " + ok);
}
```

**Stored function (returns value):**
```java
// {? = call function_name(?)} — ? before = is the return value
CallableStatement cs = conn.prepareCall("{? = call get_balance(?)}");
cs.registerOutParameter(1, Types.DECIMAL); // return value
cs.setInt(2, accountId);
cs.execute();
BigDecimal balance = cs.getBigDecimal(1);
```
